# Reads 334's artifact read-only and writes 336's re-analysis beside it. Not
# run by any workflow -- it produces a committed file that the tests pin, so it
# only runs when a person regenerates that file on purpose. Committed anyway
# because a number nobody can reproduce is a number nobody can check.
!batch-runner/scripts/reanalyse_audio_stability.py
!batch-runner/scripts/replay_audio_format_failures.py
# Run by .github/workflows/audio-format-diagnostic.yml, on the free job as well
# as the paid one. `replay_audio_format_failures.py` above answers what the
# *recorded outcomes* reclassify to; this one places the calls that produce the
# response bodies nobody stored, which is the only way to see why they failed.
!batch-runner/scripts/diagnose_audio_format_failure.py
!batch-runner/scripts/build_speech_verification_set.py
!batch-runner/scripts/check_dev_host_migration_triggers.py
!batch-runner/scripts/analyze_required_item_definition.py
# Run by .github/workflows/execution-envelope-preflight.yml. Without this line
# the directory rule above hides it, `git add -A` skips it in silence, and the
# workflow calls a file that is not in the repository -- so the job dies at the
# step whose whole purpose is to refuse an unreadable report. Held by
# tests/test_the_free_check_runs_in_ci.py, which asks git what is tracked
# rather than trusting this list to have been kept up.
!batch-runner/scripts/summarise_execution_envelope_check_in_korean.py
# The offline check that a published grade's cost receipt is backed by its own
# ledger. Same reason as the line above: the directory rule hides it, `git add`
# skips it without a word, and a receipt check that is not in the repository
# cannot be run against the next paid run. Held by
# tests/test_verify_cost_ledger.py.
!batch-runner/scripts/verify_cost_ledger.py
# The check for 340 §6.2's nine metering conditions, written before the run it
# judges so the conditions cannot be chosen after seeing the result. Different
# question from `verify_cost_ledger.py` above -- that one asks whether a
# published grade's receipt is backed by its ledger; this one asks whether an
# audio diagnostic's spend reached a ledger at all, which is what 337 turned
# out not to have done. Same reason it has to be named: the directory rule
# hides it, `git add -A` skips it in silence, and a check that is not in the
# repository cannot be run against the paid run it was written for. Held by
# tests/test_the_metering_check_can_fail.py.
!batch-runner/scripts/verify_audio_metering_run.py
# The follow-up check that runs on the commit a grading run actually pushed.
# It is invoked by the verify-published job in grade-run.yml, so the directory
# rule hiding it would leave that job calling a file the checkout does not
# have -- the exact failure this whole path exists to stop, reproduced one
# level up. Held by tests/test_verify_published_grades.py.
!batch-runner/scripts/verify_published_grades.py
# The check for 344 §6's four verdicts, written before the run it judges so
# the bar is the plan's and not the result's. Same reason it has to be named
# as the two above: the directory rule hides it, `git add -A` skips it in
# silence, and this one would fail in the worst possible way -- 344 §0 ticks a
# precondition on this file having been merged, so an untracked copy would let
# a paid dispatch clear a gate held open by a file nobody else has. Measured
# on this branch: it was written, tested green, and absent from `git status`.
# Held by tests/test_the_format_check_can_fail.py.
!batch-runner/scripts/verify_format_pilot_run.py

# The two halves of the Codex sandbox host question. The diagnostic names which
# wall a machine hits when bubblewrap will not start; the checker holds each
# host to what docs/codex_sandbox_hosts.json says about it. Both are invoked by
# codex-sandbox-host-survey.yml on GitHub-hosted runners, so the directory rule
# hiding them would leave that workflow calling files the checkout does not
# have. Held by tests/test_codex_sandbox_host_diagnosis.py.
!batch-runner/scripts/diagnose_codex_sandbox_host.py
!batch-runner/scripts/check_recorded_sandbox_verdicts.py

# The other half of the same question, and a different half entirely: whether
# the deployment answers Codex at all. Run by
# codex-foundry-connection-diagnostic.yml on a hosted runner under the
# repository's existing OIDC sign-in, so it has to survive the directory rule
# for the same reason the two above do. Held by
# tests/test_codex_foundry_connection_probe.py.
!batch-runner/scripts/diagnose_codex_foundry_connection.py

# Reads infra/dev-host/ and holds it to the Xenology card's deployment
# contract -- region, image, size, key-only access, an identity with no role,
# automatic shutdown. It runs offline and in a pull request, which is the whole
# point of it, so it cannot be a file the checkout does not have. Held by
# tests/test_dev_host_definition.py.
!batch-runner/scripts/check_dev_host_definition.py

# The only thing in the repository that spends money on Agentic Sandbox V2, and
# it spends it once. It has to be committed because it cannot be run from a
# checkout at all: the client factory refuses static credentials, so the only
# place it works is a workflow job holding a federated token -- and that job
# runs what the repository contains. Its --dry-run path is the same code minus
# the network and runs in a pull request. Held by
# tests/test_run_agentic_stage_a_probe.py.
!batch-runner/scripts/run_agentic_stage_a_probe.py


